{% extends "base.html.j2" %}
{% block title %}Methods{% endblock %}
{% block description %}How the AM Process Atlas rates additive manufacturing processes: the rubric, the bands, the process families, and where the references come from.{% endblock %}
{% block body %}
<div style="display:grid;gap:26px">
<header class="head"><h1>Methods</h1>
<p class="lede">What the ratings and bands mean, how processes are grouped, and how the atlas is sourced.</p></header>

<section class="prose">
  <h2>What this is</h2>
  <p>A reference for choosing an additive manufacturing process. Each of the {{ processes|length }} processes is rated on ten dimensions against the whole field of additive processes, so a 5 means best in additive manufacturing, not best possible. Ratings are editorial judgments anchored to the rubric below and supported by the references on each process page.</p>
  <p>Entry price, smallest feature, and largest part are shown as bands rather than single numbers, because they vary by vendor, configuration, and geometry. The detail, size, and system cost ratings are computed from those bands, so the two always agree; the other seven ratings are editorial.</p>
  <p>Each band cites the spec sheet, design guide, or price report it comes from. Prices labeled "reported" come from trade press or resellers rather than the manufacturer. Where a vendor quotes only on request, the band is an estimate and the process page says so.</p>
  <p>Vendors are listed alphabetically and are not exhaustive. Listing is not endorsement, and no vendor is featured or ranked.</p>
</section>

<section style="display:grid;gap:10px" id="rubric"><h2>Rating rubric</h2>
<div class="tframe"><table><thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>1</th><th>2</th><th>3</th><th>4</th><th>5</th></tr></thead><tbody>
{% for k, d in rubric.items() %}<tr><td style="font-weight:600;white-space:nowrap">{{ d.label }}</td>{% for i in [1,2,3,4,5] %}<td>{{ d.levels[i] }}</td>{% endfor %}</tr>{% endfor %}
</tbody></table></div></section>

<section style="display:grid;gap:10px" id="bands"><h2>Bands</h2>
<div class="tframe"><table><thead><tr><th>Attribute</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Bands</th></tr></thead><tbody>
{% for axis in ["system_price", "min_feature", "max_part"] %}<tr><td style="font-weight:600">{{ bands[axis].label }}</td><td>{{ bands[axis].note }}</td><td>{{ bands[axis].bands|map(attribute="label")|join(" · ") }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
</tbody></table></div></section>

<section style="display:grid;gap:10px"><h2>Process families</h2>
<p class="sub">After ISO/ASTM 52900, with powder bed fusion split into polymer and metal because buyers shop them separately.</p>
<div class="tframe"><table><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Family</th><th>Processes</th></tr></thead><tbody>
{% for f in family_list %}<tr><td class="band">{{ f.code }}</td><td style="font-weight:600">{{ f.name }}</td><td>{% for p in processes if p.family == f.id %}<a href="{{ p.id }}.html">{{ p.name }}</a>{% if not loop.last %} · {% endif %}{% endfor %}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
</tbody></table></div></section>

<section class="prose"><h2>Picker scoring</h2>
<p>The picker drops processes that fail a hard requirement (material, feature size, part size, budget, must-have capability), then scores the rest as the weighted average of their ten ratings, scaled 0–100. Quantity adds weight: prototypes favor low system cost and easy workflow; production favors throughput and cost per part. Emerging processes are discounted 8% and research processes 15% for maturity risk.</p></section>

<section class="prose" id="compare"><h2>Comparisons</h2>
<p>The compare page and the X-vs-Y pages show the same data as the process pages, side by side. "Where they differ" lists every band that differs and every rating that differs by 2 or more; a difference of 1 is within editorial judgment. Each X-vs-Y intro describes only how the processes work, which the cited textbook chapters cover.</p></section>

<section class="prose" id="materials"><h2>Materials explorer</h2>
<p>Each grade's values are read from a technical data sheet hosted by the vendor, linked on every row; copies hosted by resellers or service bureaus are not used. Where a vendor no longer serves a data sheet for a grade, the grade is listed as unavailable and not plotted. Grades are grouped by the use the vendor sells them for: engineering and general purpose, dental, biomedical, or castable. Dental and biomedical grades are listed for their mechanical data only; the atlas does not assess biocompatibility or regulatory status. Grades whose sheets publish only flexural or hardness data are not listed. One condition is recorded per grade: the standard post-cure, heat treatment, or finish the sheet reports, stated in the Condition column.</p>
<p>Tensile strength is the ultimate value, the larger of yield and break. XY and XZ specimens are both counted as in-plane and ZX and Z specimens as the build direction, unless the sheet names its directions in words (horizontal, vertical), which then decide. Where a sheet gives a range, the table shows the range and the chart plots its midpoint with a line across the range. Modulus is shown in GPa; the CSV keeps MPa as published.</p>
<p>Values from different vendors are not directly comparable. Test standards (ASTM D638, ISO 527, ASTM D412 for elastomers, ASTM E8 or ISO 6892 for metals), specimen geometry, print orientation, build settings, and post-processing all move the numbers, and some sheets do not state orientation. The explorer shows these per grade so a reader can judge. A sheet value is a typical or average result, not a design allowable.</p></section>

<section class="prose" id="calculator"><h2>Buy vs outsource calculator</h2>
<p>The calculator compares owning a printer with buying the same part from a service bureau, for one part design at a time. Every input comes from the reader. The fields start at placeholders, not market figures; the optional example system prices are the sourced prices from the landscape page, printer only, using the lower figure where a report gives a range. Readers can name a custom printer and save its inputs; saved printers stay in that browser and are never sent anywhere.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Fixed cost per printer per year</b> = system price ÷ depreciation years + system price × service %.</li>
<li><b>Material per part</b> = material per part (cm³) × price per cm³ × (1 + waste %). Price per cm³ is the price per liter ÷ 1,000, or the price per kg × density ÷ 1,000.</li>
<li><b>Variable cost per good part</b> = (material per part × parts per build + consumables per build + labor minutes ÷ 60 × labor rate) ÷ (parts per build × success rate). Failed parts still use material, consumables, and labor.</li>
<li><b>Capacity per printer</b> = whole builds in 8,760 h × utilization ÷ build time, × parts per build × success rate.</li>
<li><b>In-house cost per part</b> at volume V = variable cost + printers × fixed cost ÷ V, where printers = V ÷ capacity rounded up.</li>
<li><b>Break-even volume</b> = fixed cost ÷ (service quote − variable cost). There is none when the quote is at or below the variable cost, and it is out of reach when it exceeds one printer's capacity, because each added printer brings the same fixed cost.</li>
</ul>
<p>Worked example with the placeholder inputs: material is 20 cm³ × $0.10 × 1.3 = $2.60 a part. One build costs 40 × $2.60 + $20 + 60 min at $60/h = $184 for 38 good parts, or $4.84 each. Fixed cost is $50,000 ÷ 5 + 10% × $50,000 = $15,000 a year. Against a $25 quote, break-even is $15,000 ÷ ($25 − $4.84) = 744.1, so 745 parts a year. At 2,000 parts the in-house cost is $4.84 + $15,000 ÷ 2,000 = $12.34 a part.</p>
<p>Not modeled: financing and interest, floor space and facility work, operator training, design-for-AM engineering, inspection beyond the success rate, taxes, and the value of faster turnaround or keeping designs in house. The comparison holds part design and quality constant; a service bureau may run a different machine class.</p></section>

<section class="prose" id="events"><h2>Industry events</h2>
<p>The events page lists six kinds of event: system and material launches, acquisitions, mergers, funding rounds, wind-downs (bankruptcy, closure, insolvency), and market exits (a business sold or a product line discontinued). A daily job watches {{ n_watched }} companies: the vendors with regular news, plus companies that have left the market. It reads headlines from Bing News, Google News, and GDELT, the companies' own news feeds where they publish one, and SEC filings for companies that file with the SEC. Only headlines, links, dates, and publishers are kept, never article text.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Accepted automatically:</b> only funding rounds and product launches from primary sources. A funding headline needs a tier-1 source (the company's own site, a press wire, the SEC, or a short list of major outlets), exactly one US-dollar amount, a closing verb or named round, and no debt, grant, or offering wording. Launches are taken only from the company's own site or a press wire, since trade-press launch stories repeat those releases; a launch that names a printer, system, or material is accepted, and one that names only a platform goes to review. Talks and plans are never accepted automatically.</li>
<li><b>Reviewed by hand:</b> every acquisition, merger, wind-down, and market exit; everything from trade press and other secondary sources; anything that names more than one watched company; and every SEC filing. Reviewers check the source, set the date, and choose which processes the event concerns.</li>
<li><b>Duplicates:</b> a funding amount within 10% of a recorded round, or a headline that repeats a recorded event for the same company within 30 days, is closed as a duplicate.</li>
<li><b>Dates</b> are when the event happened or was announced. A month or year alone means the source gives no exact day. Events dated before the feed started were entered from a reviewed timeline with each date checked against its source.</li>
</ul>
<p>Source tiers, the watch list, and every decision (accepted, rejected, or duplicate) are in the repository. The <a href="status.html">status page</a> shows the last run of each source. An event's listing says nothing about the company's products or prospects.</p>
</section>

<section class="prose" id="claims"><h2>Checked statements</h2>
<p>Summary sentences, such as counts on the landscape page, are stored with the condition they rest on and re-checked against the data on every build. If the data changes so a sentence no longer holds, a neutral version is shown until it is rewritten. The <a href="status.html">status page</a> lists every statement and the latest link check of every cited source.</p></section>

<section class="prose"><h2>Practitioner notes</h2>
<p>Some process pages carry a short signed note from Joshua Ritchey. Notes are personal views based on public information and general experience. They discuss technologies, not vendors.</p></section>

<section class="prose"><h2>Corrections</h2>
<p>Open an issue on <a href="{{ repo }}/issues">GitHub</a> with a source. Data files are in <a href="{{ repo }}/tree/main/reference">reference/</a>.</p></section>
</div>
{% endblock %}
